1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,575
A script that always does the same thing is limited.

2
00:00:03,595 --> 00:00:08,551
Give it arguments, and one script can serve every user, every file, every server.

3
00:00:09,021 --> 00:00:13,075
In this video: process script inputs, like $1 and $2.

4
00:00:17,595 --> 00:00:20,178
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:20,648 --> 00:00:24,233
Process script inputs ($1, $2, etc.).

6
00:00:24,703 --> 00:00:29,290
We'll read arguments with $1 and $2, see the script name and the count,

7
00:00:29,310 --> 00:00:34,027
handle quotes, check that enough arguments were given, and set a default.

8
00:00:43,547 --> 00:00:46,633
When you run a script with words after its name,

9
00:00:46,653 --> 00:00:50,321
the shell hands them to the script as numbered variables.

10
00:00:50,341 --> 00:00:53,362
$1 is the first word, $2 the second, and so on.

11
00:00:59,832 --> 00:01:05,087
$0 is the script's own name. $# is how many arguments there are.

12
00:01:05,107 --> 00:01:07,065
And "$@" is all of them.

13
00:01:16,585 --> 00:01:20,846
Here is args.sh. Each echo prints one of the special variables.

14
00:01:20,866 --> 00:01:23,904
We run it with three words: red, green, blue.

15
00:01:28,374 --> 00:01:33,491
$0 is the script name, as we typed it. First is red, second is green.

16
00:01:33,511 --> 00:01:39,746
The count is 3. And all of them together. A $3 exists too; we just did not print it.

17
00:01:47,266 --> 00:01:51,065
With no arguments, the variables are empty, and the count is 0.

18
00:01:54,535 --> 00:01:58,075
If one argument has a space in it, put quotes around it.

19
00:01:58,095 --> 00:02:00,999
The shell then passes it as a single argument:

20
00:02:01,019 --> 00:02:03,987
red green is the first one, and the count is 2.

21
00:02:10,507 --> 00:02:14,553
Real scripts check their input. greet.sh tests the count:

22
00:02:14,573 --> 00:02:19,476
if the number of arguments is less than 1, it prints a usage message,

23
00:02:19,496 --> 00:02:24,898
and runs exit 1. The exit command stops the script and sets its exit status.

24
00:02:24,918 --> 00:02:26,039
1 means failure.

25
00:02:30,509 --> 00:02:35,637
Without a name: the usage message, and echo $? shows 1. With a name:

26
00:02:35,657 --> 00:02:38,212
the greeting, and the status is 0.

27
00:02:45,732 --> 00:02:50,683
A default value. Dollar, curly brace, 1, colon, dash, world, closing brace.

28
00:02:50,703 --> 00:02:55,653
It means: use the first argument, and if there is none, use the word world.

29
00:03:00,123 --> 00:03:03,581
Without an argument: Hello, world. With Ana: Hello, Ana.

30
00:03:09,101 --> 00:03:12,537
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

31
00:03:14,057 --> 00:03:17,115
Scripts are files on disk, so they survive a reboot.

32
00:03:17,135 --> 00:03:19,541
We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

33
00:03:23,011 --> 00:03:27,812
After a fresh boot, ./args.sh with three words works the same way.

34
00:03:35,332 --> 00:03:41,089
Your turn. Make a folder called lab18. In it, create an executable script, who.sh.

35
00:03:41,109 --> 00:03:45,880
Given a name and a city, it prints the name, lives in, and the city.

36
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:51,376
With fewer than two arguments, it prints a Usage line and exits with status 1.

37
00:03:57,846 --> 00:04:00,663
Run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

38
00:04:06,183 --> 00:04:09,640
Next up: processing the output of shell commands within a script.

39
00:04:10,110 --> 00:04:13,013
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
